  "title": "Listening to Understand, Not to React",
  "content": "Children communicate through words, behaviour, and emotion. Listening deeply means paying attention to what your child is trying to express—not just the surface behaviour. When parents listen with curiosity instead of judgment, children feel understood and are more willing to share.\n\nThis lesson teaches parents how to slow down, reflect back what they hear, and validate their child’s feelings. Validation does not mean agreeing with everything; it means acknowledging the child’s experience. When children feel heard, their emotional intensity decreases and cooperation increases.\n\nParents also learn how to avoid common communication blocks such as interrupting, lecturing, or dismissing feelings.",
  "activities": [
    "During your next conversation with your child, reflect back one thing they said using the phrase 'What I hear you saying is…'",
    "Practice listening for one full minute without interrupting, correcting, or giving advice."
  ],
  "reflectionQuestions": [
    "How does your child behave when they feel unheard?",
    "What makes it difficult for you to listen without reacting?",
    "How does your child respond when you validate their feelings?"
  ]
